chapters":["Sección 3.6"],"pages":[[1,62,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":62,"text":"…Wyatt, Ayers, Hopper and their new friends Daevid Allen and Mike Ratledge formed the Soft Machine, whereas Sinclair and the others went on to form Caravan. Soft Machine (103), one of the greatest rock bands of all times, debuted with albums such as 2 (1969) that were inspired to psychedelic-rock with a…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":62,"text":"…Virtually (1971), on their fourth album, and the futuristic 1983 (1972), on their sixth album). The other co-founders of the Canterbury school, Caravan (3), impersonated a simpler, lighter, mellower and catchier kind of jazz-rock than Soft Machine's. Their specialty were melodic fantasias that…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":64,"text":"…and Dave Stewart joined forces and formed Hatfield & The North (1), whose first album, Hatfield And The North (1974), was a competent appendix to Caravan. Then Dave Stewart and Alan Gowen formed a more keyboard-oriented band, the National Health (2), who were not shy to toy with dissonance,…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":181866284665489,"type":"album","name":"Caravan","list_id":116078530059197,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Canterbury Scene","src":"musthear","rank":33,"year":1969,"note":null,"yt":"ik8dE1-SQtg","scaruffi":null},{"id":233021246031352,"type":"album","name":"If I Could Do It All Over Again, I'd Do It All Over You","list_id":116078530059197,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Canterbury Scene","src":"musthear","rank":4,"year":1970,"note":null,"yt":"ci3amN12HcE","scaruffi":null},{"id":233021246031352,"type":"album","name":"If I Could Do It All Over Again, I'd Do It All Over You","list_id":11393629798029,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Progressive Rock","src":"musthear","rank":65,"year":1970,"note":null,"yt":"ci3amN12HcE","sca